### Why the custom style draws nothing

When you point a style at a **vector tile source** you must tell every style layer the exact **`source-layer`** name that exists in the tileset.  
The hosted style URL already contains those names, so it works out-of-the-box. In your own style you’re probably using a different (or missing) `source-layer`, which makes the layer silently draw nothing.

### Fix it

1. **Reference the TileJSON, not a hand-wired template**  
   ```js
   map.addSource('basemap', {
     type: 'vector',
     url: 'https://tiles.example.com/tileset.json'   // TileJSON endpoint
   });
   ```
   *Using `url` lets MapLibre read the tile URL template, `minzoom`/`maxzoom`, and – most importantly – the list of `vector_layers` that tells you the exact `source-layer` names.*

2. **Find the correct `source-layer` names**  
   * Open the TileJSON (`…/tileset.json`) in a browser or fetch it with `curl`.  
   * Look at the `vector_layers` array; each entry has a `id` – that is the name you must use. Example:  

   ```json
   "vector_layers": [
     { "id": "transportation", "minzoom": 5, "maxzoom": 14, "fields": { … } },
     { "id": "water",          "minzoom": 0, "maxzoom": 14, "fields": { … } },
     …
   ]
   ```

3. **Write your layers with the exact `source-layer`**  

   ```js
   map.addLayer({
     id: 'my-roads',
     type: 'line',
     source: 'basemap',
     'source-layer': 'transportation',   // <-- must match TileJSON id
     paint: {
       'line-color': '#ff6600',
       'line-width': 2
     }
   });
   ```

4. **Insert your layers *below* the first symbol layer (labels)**  
   If you add layers after everything else they will sit on top of the basemap’s labels and appear to hide the map. Do:

   ```js
   // Find the first symbol layer (usually a label layer)
   const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

   // Add your custom layer before that layer
   map.addLayer(myLayerObject, firstSymbol);
   ```

5. **Check CORS (only if the TileJSON or tiles are on another origin)**  
   The tile server must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain). If the request is blocked you’ll see it in the Network tab; the map will be blank.

### Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Custom style on hosted tiles</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
const map = new maplibre.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      basemap: {
        type: 'vector',
        url: 'https://tiles.example.com/tileset.json'   // <-- TileJSON
      }
    },
    layers: []   // start empty; we’ll add our own layers later
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

map.on('load', () => {
  // Find first symbol layer (labels) to insert before
  const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

  // Add a road line layer
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-roads',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'basemap',
    'source-layer': 'transportation',   // <-- exact name from TileJSON
    paint: {
      'line-color': '#ff6600',
      'line-width': 2
    }
  }, firstSymbol);
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Quick checklist

| What to verify | How |
|----------------|-----|
| **Source definition** – use `url` to the TileJSON (or correctly-wired `tiles` with `minzoom`/`maxzoom`). | `type: 'vector', url: '…/tileset.json'` |
| **`source-layer` names** – match the `id` fields in `vector_layers` of the TileJSON. | Open TileJSON, copy the exact string. |
| **Layer order** – insert before the first `symbol` layer so you don’t cover labels. | `map.addLayer(layerObj, firstSymbolId)` |
| **CORS** – server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. | Check Network tab for blocked requests. |

Once the `source-layer` names line up and the layers are inserted below the label layer, your custom style will render on top of the hosted basemap just like the provider’s style URL does.

Add the layer **before** the first symbol-type layer (the layers that draw street names, place labels, POI icons, etc.).  
`map.addLayer()` can take a second argument – the id of the layer that should stay **above** the new one.  
If you pass the id of the first symbol layer, your data will be rendered underneath all of the labels.

```js
// 1️⃣ Find the first symbol layer in the current style
let firstSymbolId = null;
for (const layer of map.getStyle().layers) {
  if (layer.type === 'symbol') {
    firstSymbolId = layer.id;
    break;          // stop at the first symbol layer
  }
}

// 2️⃣ Add your GeoJSON source (if you haven’t already)
map.addSource('my-data', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: myGeojson
});

// 3️⃣ Add the layer, inserting it below the labels
map.addLayer(
  {
    id: 'my-data-fill',
    type: 'fill',          // or line, circle, etc.
    source: 'my-data',
    paint: {
      'fill-color': '#ff6600',
      'fill-opacity': 0.4
    }
  },
  firstSymbolId   // ← second argument: insert below this layer
);
```

**Why this works**

- MapLibre draws layers in the order they appear in the style.  
- When you call `addLayer()` without a second argument, the layer is appended **on top of everything**, which hides the street-name symbols.  
- Supplying the id of the first symbol layer tells MapLibre to place your new layer **just before** that symbol layer, so all subsequent symbol layers (labels, icons, shields) are rendered on top of your data.

If you already know the exact id of a label layer (e.g., `"road-label"` or `"place-label"` from the basemap you’re using), you can pass that id directly instead of searching for the first symbol layer. The programmatic search above works for any style, even when the label layer ids differ between basemaps.

You’re almost there – the only thing that stops the OSM tiles from lining up with the rest of the map is the **default tile size**.  
MapLibre assumes raster tiles are 512 px unless you tell it otherwise, and the public OSM tile server serves 256 px PNGs. Add `tileSize: 256` (and, for good practice, an attribution) when you create the source, then add a raster layer that points at that source.

```js
// 1️⃣  Add the raster source
map.addSource('osm-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [
    'https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  ],
  tileSize: 256,          // <-- required for OSM’s 256 px tiles
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 19,            // OSM’s published zoom range
  attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors'
});

// 2️⃣  Add a raster layer that uses the source
map.addLayer({
  id: 'osm-basemap',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'osm-basemap',
  // Insert it below the first symbol layer so you don’t cover labels
  // (optional – you can also let it be the bottom layer)
}, firstSymbolLayerId);   // replace with the id of the first symbol layer in your style
```

**What this does**

| Piece | Why it’s needed |
|------|-----------------|
| `tileSize: 256` | Aligns the raster tiles with the map’s zoom levels (default 512 would make the imagery appear at the wrong scale). |
| `minzoom` / `maxzoom` | Gives MapLibre the correct zoom range; OSM tiles exist from 0 to 19. |
| `attribution` | Required by the OSM licence and also shows up in the map’s built-in attribution control. |
| Inserting the layer **below** the first `symbol` layer (or any other label layer) prevents the raster from covering street names and other labels. |

That’s all you need for a working OSM raster basemap in MapLibre. Once the source and layer are added, the tiles will load and display correctly.

**What’s happening**

`setFeatureState` attaches a piece of state to a *feature id*.  
Your GeoJSON features only have the identifier inside `properties.parcel_id`.  
Because the source was added without telling MapLibre which property should be
used as the feature **id**, every feature’s id is `undefined`.  
When you call

```js
map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: '12345' }, { hover: true })
```

MapLibre can’t find a feature with that id, so the call silently does nothing,
`getFeatureState` returns `{}` and the paint expression that looks at
`feature-state` never fires.  

The fix is to **promote** the `parcel_id` property to the feature id when the
source is created (or generate stable ids yourself). Once the source has a
proper id, `setFeatureState` works exactly as the docs describe.

---

## Step-by-step wiring

### 1. Add the GeoJSON source with `promoteId`

```js
map.addSource('parcels', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: parcelsGeojson,          // your FeatureCollection
  promoteId: 'parcel_id'        // <-- the property that holds the id
});
```

* `promoteId` can be a string (applies to every source-layer) or an object
  `{ <sourceLayer>: <propertyName> }` for vector sources.  
* The promoted value may be a string, number, or any primitive – it does **not**
  have to be an integer.

### 2. Add a layer that uses `feature-state` for the hover style

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'parcels-fill',
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'parcels',
  paint: {
    // default fill
    'fill-color': '#88c0d0',
    // when hover state is true, use a brighter colour
    'fill-color': [
      'case',
      ['boolean', ['feature-state', 'hover'], false],
      '#ffcc00',          // hover colour
      '#88c0d0'           // normal colour
    ],
    'fill-opacity': 0.6
  }
});
```

### 3. Wire the mouse events

```js
let hoveredId = null;

// mouse move → set hover state
map.on('mousemove', 'parcels-fill', e => {
  if (e.features.length === 0) return;

  const feature = e.features[0];
  const id = feature.id;               // <-- this is the promoted parcel_id

  if (hoveredId !== null && hoveredId !== id) {
    // clear previous hover
    map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
  }

  // set new hover
  map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id }, { hover: true });
  hoveredId = id;
});

// mouse leave → clear hover
map.on('mouseleave', 'parcels-fill', () => {
  if (hoveredId !== null) {
    map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
    hoveredId = null;
  }
});
```

**Why we use `feature.id`**  
After `promoteId` is set, MapLibre copies the value of `properties.parcel_id`
onto the top-level `id` field of each feature. That is the identifier that
`setFeatureState` expects.

### 4. (Optional) Verify the state works

```js
// after hovering a parcel
const state = map.getFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId });
console.log(state);   // → { hover: true }
```

If you see the expected object, the wiring is correct.

---

## Common pitfalls that cause the same symptom

| Symptom | Typical cause | Fix |
|---------|---------------|-----|
| `setFeatureState` does nothing, no console error | No `id` on the feature (missing `promoteId` or `generateId`) | Add `promoteId` (or `generateId: true` **only** if you don’t have a stable business key) |
| Hover works on the first feature but then “jumps” after `setData` | Using `generateId:true` – ids are re-assigned on each `setData` | Use `promoteId` instead of `generateId` |
| State never changes, but `feature-state` expression works when you manually set `id` in the GeoJSON | You added a top-level `id` that isn’t the same type as the value you later pass to `setFeatureState` | Ensure the id you pass matches the promoted id exactly (string vs number) |
| No hover on a **vector** source | Forgot `source-layer` in the layer definition **and** didn’t promote the id per source-layer | Add `source-layer` and `promoteId: { '<layerName>': 'parcel_id' }` |

---

## TL;DR checklist

1. **Add the source with `promoteId: 'parcel_id'`.**  
2. Use a layer that references the source (no `source-layer` needed for GeoJSON).  
3. In mouse handlers, call `setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: feature.id }, …)`.  
4. Verify with `getFeatureState`.  

Once the source knows which property is the feature id, `setFeatureState`
behaves exactly as documented and your hover highlight will appear.
